            <ArticleTitle>Interrupting synoviolin play at the ER: a plausible action to elevate mitochondrial energetics and silence obesity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>981-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.15252/embj.201591240</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Obesity is a global concern, which has been linked to increased risk for cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, atherosclerosis,non-alcoholic fatty liver, and cancer. In this issue of The EMBO Journal,Fujita et al (2015) describe the role of an endoplasmic reticulum (ER)-resident E3ubiquitin ligase, synoviolin, and its ability to control body weight and energy expenditure by targeting PGC-1b, a transcriptional modulator of mitochondrial oxidative metabolism.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
